    <DataNode elementId="AT1G05620-MONOMER" textLabel="AT1G05620-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT1G05620-MONOMER" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="775" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">NSH2 appears to act as an inosine-specific purine nucleosidase. Attempts to express it in a heterologous system for enzymatic characterization failed, but, overexpression of the NSH2 gene in |FRAME: ORG-5993| led to more than a doubling of the inosine hydrolysis activity. No other pyrimidine or purine tested in hydrolysis assays appeared to be used as substrates |CITS: [21235647]|. Transient over-expression of a GFP-tagged version of NSH2 in onion epidermal cells indicates that this enzyme localizes to the cytoplasm. Transcript expression analyses reveal an increase in NSH2 levels in older leaves suggesting that this protein may play a role in purine catabolic processes during leaf senescence |CITS: [21235647]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G05620-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33090, RXN-12883" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="GOTerms" value="GO:0005829" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT1G05620.1" />
