    <DataNode elementId="AT3G53900-MONOMER" textLabel="AT3G53900-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9M336" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="855" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Six homologous genes are predicted to encode proteins with uracil phosphoribosyltransferase (UPRT) domains in |FRAME: TAX-3702 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;|. Five of the six genes are very similar to each other, and encode dual-domain proteins, with the N-terminal encoding a uridine kinase. These genes were named |FRAME: AT5G40870 UKL1| through UKL5 (for UK-like). The 6th gene, |FRAME: AT3G53900 UPP|, encodes a mono-domain UPRT protein. Assays performed with all six genes and |FRAME: TAX-562 &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;| mutants lacking the genes |FRAME: EG10809 pyrF| (responsible for the last step of the de novo pyrimidine synthesis pathway), |FRAME: EG11045 udp| (uridine phosphorylase) and |FRAME: EG11332 upp| (UPRT) tested which of these genes could enable the mutant to grow with |FRAME: URACIL uracil| as the sole pyrimidine sourcere. The results showed clearly that only the |FRAME: AT3G53900 UPP| gene was able to restore such growth |CITS: [19563437]|. Furthermore, characterization of T-DNA insertion mutants for the |FRAME: AT3G53900 UPP|, |FRAME: AT5G40870 UKL1|, UKL2 and UKL3 genes showed that the disruption of the single |FRAME: AT3G53900 UPP| gene is sufficient to abolish UPRT function in |FRAME: TAX-3702 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;| |CITS: [19563437]|. Thus, it is believed that the UPRT domains of the UKL1-UKL5 genes are inactive, and that UPP is the only active UPRT in |FRAME: TAX-3702 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;|. Using a translational fusion between the first 125 amino acids of the UPP protein and the green fluorescent protein it was shown that the protein is targeted to plastids |CITS: [19563437]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G53900-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT3G53900.2" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Phylomedb" value="Q9M336" />
